    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-115013",
      "sequence_position": 11,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/8536196127d1e8fc92cd2d442005a386b2d981a027a497ecce363559f52ac0e6.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4?$",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=-\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{5}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{5}{2}f(x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/bafacd18cae6114ad22fb59d905a8ebb68cc8768a5b20a4a5a2e36f59c11ad11.png)\n\n- Then, reflect the result across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{5}{2}f(x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/bd69db18de3d58e6806b7ee3473a0fd4e8b6df384cf371be4e9648cac53e3927.png)\n\n- Finally, translate the result down by $4$ units to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/fe2e7245978e120a538cc87a31d6180c4e342fc438240be1f3ce367eed64bb33.png)",
